---
export const prerender = true;
import CalculatorGuideArticle from '../../components/CalculatorGuideArticle.astro';
---

<CalculatorGuideArticle
  toolSlug="matrix-calculator"
  label="Matrix calculator guide"
  title="Matrix Calculator Guide"
  description="Check 2x2 and 3x3 matrix addition, multiplication, transpose, and determinant steps with plain examples."
  path="/blog/how-to-use-matrix-calculator/"
  intro="Matrix work gets messy when one row or column is copied wrong. This guide shows what to enter, what the calculator is doing, and which result checks matter most."
  quickStart={[
    'Choose 2x2 or 3x3.',
    'Choose the operation you want.',
    'Enter Matrix A. Enter Matrix B too for add, subtract, and multiply.',
    'Press Calculate matrix, then check the result, steps, examples, and recent answers.',
  ]}
  sections={[
    {
      title: 'Fast 2x2 checks',
      paragraphs: [
        'For addition, match the same position in each matrix. [[1,2],[3,4]] plus [[5,6],[7,8]] becomes [[6,8],[10,12]]. Nothing moves around.',
        'For a 2x2 determinant, use ad - bc. Matrix [[3,4],[2,5]] gives 3x5 - 4x2 = 7. If you get a different number, check the diagonal order first.',
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Matrix multiplication order',
      paragraphs: [
        'Matrix multiplication uses rows from Matrix A and columns from Matrix B. That is why A x B can be different from B x A.',
        'Example: [[1,2],[3,4]] x [[2,0],[1,2]] gives [[4,4],[10,8]]. The top-left answer is 1x2 + 2x1 = 4.',
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Determinant and transpose',
      paragraphs: [
        'A determinant is one number from a square matrix. A 3x3 determinant needs more steps, so use the calculator output as a check while you study the expansion.',
        'Transpose swaps rows and columns. Entry row 1, column 2 becomes row 2, column 1. This page keeps the grid at 2x2 and 3x3 so it stays readable on a phone.',
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Mistakes to check first',
      paragraphs: [
        'Addition and subtraction need the same matrix size. If the problem mixes sizes, the operation is not defined.',
        'Do not flip multiplication order unless your teacher or source says to. Matrix A times Matrix B is a different question from Matrix B times Matrix A.',
      ],
      links: [
        {
          href: 'https://openstax.org/books/college-algebra-2e/pages/7-5-matrices-and-matrix-operations',
          label: 'OpenStax: matrices and matrix operations',
        },
        {
          href: 'https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content',
          label: 'Google Search Central: helpful, people-first content',
        },
      ],
    },
  ]}
  sidecarText="Open the Matrix Calculator beside this guide and test the 2x2 examples as you read."
  bestUsesIntro="Use this page when the problem is about small square matrices and you want to check the answer without losing the row and column logic."
  keepExploringIntro="If the matrix problem is only part of the assignment, these links keep you close to algebra and calculator pages that match it."
/>
